1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,776
When an AI agent decides to do something, what actually decides whether it is allowed to happen?

2
00:00:09,026 --> 00:00:12,381
That question is behind this whole project.

3
00:00:12,431 --> 00:00:13,807
It's called Atlas.

4
00:00:18,557 --> 00:00:21,150
Atlas looks like an ordinary assistant.

5
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,488
You type a request, and it can use tools: read a project record,

6
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:29,827
open a document, fetch updates from a plugin, or send a message.

7
00:00:31,477 --> 00:00:35,344
Everything here is a controlled, synthetic environment.

8
00:00:35,394 --> 00:00:41,398
The documents are fictional, and a message is only ever added to an in-memory outbox.

9
00:00:44,948 --> 00:00:48,068
Let's build the flow, one piece at a time.

10
00:00:48,118 --> 00:00:50,937
It starts with you, sending a request.

11
00:00:52,587 --> 00:00:54,650
The request reaches a model.

12
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:59,026
The model reads it, and may decide that a tool would help.

13
00:01:00,876 --> 00:01:03,663
When it does, it doesn't run the tool.

14
00:01:03,713 --> 00:01:07,845
It produces a proposal: a tool name, and some arguments.

15
00:01:07,895 --> 00:01:10,011
That is all the model can do.

16
00:01:13,061 --> 00:01:14,531
Now the important part.

17
00:01:14,581 --> 00:01:17,901
Between that proposal and the tool sits the server.

18
00:01:17,951 --> 00:01:21,999
This is trusted code that we wrote, and it makes the decision.

19
00:01:25,049 --> 00:01:28,351
The server checks the call against policy.

20
00:01:28,401 --> 00:01:32,262
If it's allowed, the tool runs on synthetic data.

21
00:01:32,312 --> 00:01:33,938
If not, nothing runs.

22
00:01:33,988 --> 00:01:36,571
Either way, an event is recorded.

23
00:01:41,621 --> 00:01:45,031
Now let's see the difference in the real application.

24
00:01:45,081 --> 00:01:50,841
These are scripted scenarios, not the live model, so they behave the same way every time.

25
00:01:52,691 --> 00:01:57,171
With Security off, a plugin update contains a hidden instruction.

26
00:01:57,221 --> 00:02:03,652
The scripted agent follows it, reads a restricted memo, and queues it for an outside address.

27
00:02:03,702 --> 00:02:07,415
Nothing is really sent, but nothing stopped it either.

28
00:02:10,465 --> 00:02:12,969
Now the same request with Security on.

29
00:02:13,019 --> 00:02:16,059
The plugin is blocked, because it is unsigned.

30
00:02:16,109 --> 00:02:20,696
The agent finishes the task from the verified project record instead.

31
00:02:23,746 --> 00:02:27,629
A live model might not fall for the hidden instruction at all.

32
00:02:27,679 --> 00:02:33,021
That is why the vulnerable behaviour is scripted: it makes the comparison repeatable.

33
00:02:38,071 --> 00:02:39,439
Now a live request.

34
00:02:39,489 --> 00:02:45,261
I'm asking the real model, running on Groq, to read the compensation document,

35
00:02:45,311 --> 00:02:48,695
and I claim to be the chief financial officer.

36
00:02:51,545 --> 00:02:54,199
The model proposes reading that document.

37
00:02:54,249 --> 00:02:58,024
The server compares the call with the agent's permissions.

38
00:02:58,074 --> 00:03:02,112
The scope for that document is missing, so the call is denied.

39
00:03:05,162 --> 00:03:07,041
Notice what did not happen.

40
00:03:07,091 --> 00:03:09,398
Nobody asked the model to behave.

41
00:03:09,448 --> 00:03:15,898
A claim made in the prompt carries no authority, because the decision lives in server code.

42
00:03:20,948 --> 00:03:24,775
Some actions are allowed, but only with a human decision.

43
00:03:24,825 --> 00:03:27,836
Sending a message to the team is one of them.

44
00:03:29,286 --> 00:03:33,108
The server pauses the action and shows an approval card.

45
00:03:33,158 --> 00:03:34,629
Nothing has been sent.

46
00:03:37,479 --> 00:03:41,886
If I deny, the action is recorded as declined and never runs.

47
00:03:41,936 --> 00:03:47,293
If I approve, the server checks that the approval belongs to this session,

48
00:03:47,343 --> 00:03:51,165
then re-runs the full set of checks before executing.

49
00:03:54,215 --> 00:03:58,642
And remember, a send here only writes to a simulated outbox.

50
00:03:58,692 --> 00:04:01,552
No message ever leaves the application.

51
00:04:06,102 --> 00:04:08,172
Next, a question about failure.

52
00:04:08,222 --> 00:04:12,821
What should happen if the authorization check itself can't complete?

53
00:04:14,471 --> 00:04:17,470
In this scenario we simulate that outage.

54
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:24,758
With Security on, the system fails closed: if a required check can't finish, the action is denied.

55
00:04:27,208 --> 00:04:30,763
Without protection, the same outage fails open.

56
00:04:30,813 --> 00:04:33,984
That is the weakness the comparison shows.

57
00:04:37,034 --> 00:04:44,255
In the evidence panel we can see each control, its decision, a reason code, and the matching audit record.

58
00:04:44,305 --> 00:04:47,136
The record states what the server decided.

59
00:04:47,186 --> 00:04:50,017
It does not depend on what the model said.

60
00:04:53,067 --> 00:04:55,906
This was a controlled, simulated failure.

61
00:04:55,956 --> 00:05:01,050
It does not prove how the system behaves in a real infrastructure outage.

62
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:08,078
So, what did Atlas demonstrate?

63
00:05:09,528 --> 00:05:11,840
A model's output is a proposal.

64
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:14,964
Server-side policy decides what executes.

65
00:05:15,014 --> 00:05:18,012
Sensitive actions can wait for a person.

66
00:05:18,062 --> 00:05:21,137
A failed check must not allow the action.

67
00:05:21,187 --> 00:05:24,337
And audit evidence explains what happened.

68
00:05:27,887 --> 00:05:31,915
What it does not show: this is not a certified product,

69
00:05:31,965 --> 00:05:39,106
it has not had an independent penetration test, and prompt-injection detection is only heuristic.

70
00:05:39,156 --> 00:05:45,111
On this deployment, approval replay protection and audit are per server instance.

71
00:05:48,161 --> 00:05:51,268
You can try the live demo and read the code on GitHub.

72
00:05:51,318 --> 00:05:52,437
Thanks for watching.
